.Dd $Mdocdate: November 5 2026 $
.Dt SIGNET-CONFIG.YAML 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm signet-config.yaml
.Nd signet configuration file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the configuration file for
.Xr signet 1 .
It is written in YAML format.
.Pp
The packages install it as
.Pa /etc/signet/config.yaml
on
.Ox
and Debian, and as
.Pa /usr/local/etc/signet/config.yaml
on
.Fx ,
and that is where their
.Xr signet 1
looks without
.Fl config .
A build from source looks for
.Dq config.yaml
in the current directory.
.Sh FORMAT
All fields are optional except
.Va listen
and, for
.Cm signet serve ,
.Va cookie_secret .
Defaults are given with each field.
.Pp
A key that is not described here stops
.Xr signet 1
from loading the file, with an error naming the key and its line,
at the top level and inside a block alike.
So does a name under
.Va auth_methods
other than
.Li password , webauthn , email_pin
and
.Li totp ,
or a value there other than
.Dq enabled
and
.Dq disabled .
A misspelt setting would otherwise leave its default in force.
The old keys
.Li listen_addr
(now
.Va listen ) ,
.Li webauthn_enabled ,
.Li email_pin_auth
and
.Li log_file
still load,
and
.Cm signet serve
warns about the last three.
.Pp
The configuration is organized into the following sections:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li listen
The address to listen on.
Three shapes:
.Ic 1.2.3.4:9000
(an IP and port; bracketed IPv6 such as
.Ql [::]:9090
included),
.Ic :9000
(port only, all interfaces), or an absolute path such as
.Pa /var/www/signet/signet.sock
to bind a Unix domain socket.
A bare port
.Pq no colon at all
is normalised to
.Ql :\&port .
Relative paths do not select a socket.
Default:
.Dq :8080 .
.It Li protocol
The protocol served on
.Va listen :
.Ic http
(default) or
.Ic fastcgi .
The FastCGI protocol exists for web servers that speak FastCGI but cannot
reverse-proxy HTTP — notably
.Xr httpd 8
on
.Ox ,
where it lets signet share one origin with the site.
It works on either listen shape: a Unix socket path (typical) or a TCP
address.
The web server sends
.Ev HTTPS=on
for TLS connections, so requests behind it are treated as secure.
.It Li base_path
The path every route of this instance is under, such as
.Dq /comments-auth ,
so that several instances can share a host, each with its own users, database,
hook and settings.
The login page is then
.Pa /comments-auth/login ,
the health check
.Pa /comments-auth/health ,
and so on.
It has a leading slash and no trailing one, and each part holds only letters,
digits and
.Dq .\& _ ~ - .
The web server passes the path whole, without removing the base; anything
outside the base answers 404, as does a path with
.Dq .. ,
.Dq .\&
or
.Dq // .
Pages, redirects, mail links and the script all name the base.
Instances on one host must have different
.Va cookie_name
values, since the cookie's path stays
.Pa /
so that the site's applications receive it.
Default: empty, the root.
.It Li socket_mode
Octal file mode for the socket file when
.Va listen
is a path.
Default:
.Dq 0660 .
A reverse proxy running as a different user needs write permission: put the
socket in a directory the proxy can traverse, or open the mode
(e.g.\&
.Dq 0666 )
and let file-system permissions be the access boundary.
A web server reaching signet over this socket must be named in
.Li trusted_proxies ,
or every client shares one rate-limit budget.
.It Li trusted_proxies
The peers whose
.Ev X-Forwarded-For
header is believed: IP addresses, CIDR ranges, or
.Dq unix
for a peer on a unix socket.
The header is read from the right, and the first address that is not a trusted
proxy
is the client, which the rate limits and the request log use.
From any other peer the header is ignored,
because a client can send anything in it.
With
.Li protocol: fastcgi
leave this empty: the web server reports the client in the protocol itself.
Do not list
.Dq unix
if local users who should not be able to choose their rate-limit budget can
connect to
the socket.
Default: empty.
.It Li templates_dir
Path to the HTML templates directory.
The server refuses to start if a file in it cannot be read or parsed,
or if there is
no login page.
Default:
.Pa /etc/signet/templates .
.It Li static_dir
Path to the static files directory (JavaScript, CSS, images).
Default:
.Pa /etc/signet/static .
.It Li database_path
Path to the SQLite3 database file for sessions, WebAuthn credentials,
and clients.
It holds only SHA-256 hashes of session tokens,
so a copy of it gives no working sessions,
and removed rows are overwritten.
Expired sessions and PINs are removed at startup and every hour.
A PIN stays until 30 minutes after it expires,
because failed guesses are counted on
it.
A new database is created with mode 0600; an existing one keeps its mode.
Default:
.Pa /var/lib/signet/sessions.db .
.It Li csv_path
Path to the CSV file containing user accounts.
Signet replaces this file by rename, keeping its owner and mode,
so it needs write
access to the directory as well, and the file cannot be a single-file mount.
It also takes its write lock on the directory.
Default:
.Pa /var/lib/signet/users.csv .
.El
.Pp
The following fields control password hashing:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li password_hash_algorithm
Hash algorithm for new users.
Must be
.Dq bcrypt
or
.Dq argon2id .
Default:
.Dq argon2id .
.It Li bcrypt_cost
Cost factor for bcrypt hashing.
Must be between 4 and 31.
Default: 12.
.It Li argon2id_time
Number of iterations for argon2id, 1 to 64.
Default: 1.
.It Li argon2id_memory
Memory usage in kilobytes for argon2id,
from 8 times
.Li argon2id_parallelism
to 4194304 (4 GB).
Default: 65536 (64 MB).
.It Li argon2id_parallelism
Parallelism (threads) for argon2id, 1 to 255.
Default: 4.
.It Li argon2id_salt_length
Salt length in bytes for argon2id, at least 8.
Default: 16.
.It Li argon2id_key_length
Key length in bytes for argon2id, at least 16.
Default: 32.
.Pp
.Xr signet 1
will not start with argon2id settings outside these ranges.
An argon2id hash in
.Pa users.csv
with parameters outside them,
or in another form than
.Li $argon2id$v=19$m=...,t=...,p=...$salt$key ,
cannot be checked:
its password is refused like a wrong one,
and a warning names the account.
.El
.Pp
The following fields control authentication behavior:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li self_registration
Whether self-registration is allowed.
When not
.Dq enabled ,
.Cm POST /register
answers 403 and the login page does not link to it.
Must be
.Dq enabled
or
.Dq disabled .
Default:
.Dq disabled .
.It Li email_verification
How self-registration proves an address.
With
.Dq required ,
.Cm /register
takes only an address and mails a link, and the account is created when the link
is used, with the password chosen there.
Registration stays off, with a warning, until
.Va public_url
is set.
With
.Dq disabled ,
it creates the account at once: nothing proves the address, anyone can register
someone else's, and the reply shows which addresses have accounts.
It does not affect accounts made with
.Cm user-add .
Default:
.Dq required .
.It Li registration_link_ttl
Seconds a registration link works.
Default: 86400.
.It Li self_registration_approval
Whether self-registered users require approval.
While
.Dq required ,
an account with the
.Dq self_registered
flag and without
.Dq approved
cannot sign in by any method until
.Cm signet user-approve
has run.
Must be
.Dq required
or
.Dq auto_approve .
Default:
.Dq required .
.It Li multi_device
Not implemented yet: accepted and ignored.
With
.Dq disabled ,
a new sign-in is meant to end the account's other sessions.
Must be
.Dq enabled
or
.Dq disabled .
Default:
.Dq enabled .
.It Li password_recovery
Whether a mailed link can reset a password.
Must be
.Dq enabled
or
.Dq disabled .
It stays off, with a warning, until
.Va public_url
is set, and while
.Li auth_methods.password
is disabled.
Whoever reads an account's mailbox can then take the account; on an instance
with email PIN sign-in on, they already could.
Default:
.Dq disabled .
.It Li public_url
The address of this instance as a browser reaches it, such as
.Dq https://signet.example.com ,
for links in mail.
It is never taken from a request, because a forged
.Li Host
would put an attacker's site in a real message.
Links are this address, then
.Va base_path ,
then the page; with
.Va base_path
set, it must have no path of its own.
Default: empty.
.It Li second_factor
.Dq optional
or
.Dq required .
Optional, an account takes a second step only if it has an authenticator app,
and a security key is asked to verify its user where it can: a key without a
PIN signs in on possession alone.
Required, every sign-in proves two factors:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
a security key must verify its user, with a PIN or a fingerprint, both when it
is added and at each sign-in, and then signs in by itself;
keys that cannot, such as U2F-only keys, stop working;
.It
a password or a mailed PIN is followed by a code from the account's app, or,
after a password with
.Li email_pin
listed, by a mailed PIN;
.It
an account whose second factor is a key is told to sign in with it;
.It
an account with none adds an app or a key, from those listed, before the
sign-in finishes.
Whoever has only its password can do that, so an administrator who wants
better gives each new account a key first, with
.Nm signet Cm webauthn-invite .
.El
The account page will not remove an account's last app or key.
Default:
.Dq optional .
.It Li second_factor_methods
What may follow a password or a mailed PIN, and what an account may add:
.Dq totp ,
.Dq webauthn
and
.Dq email_pin .
Each one listed must be enabled in
.Li auth_methods
while
.Li second_factor
is required.
.Dq email_pin
is allowed, and
.Cm serve
warns about it: password recovery is by mail too, so whoever holds the mailbox
can sign in with it alone.
NIST SP 800-63B \(sc5.1.3.3 does not accept email as a second factor.
Default:
.Li [totp, webauthn] .
.It Li totp_issuer
The name an authenticator app shows for a key.
It cannot contain a colon.
Default:
.Dq Signet .
.It Li account_reauth_window
Seconds a sign-in counts as recent.
An older session can see
.Pa /account
and
.Pa /webauthn/keys ,
but must sign in again before changing anything there.
Default: 600.
.It Li recovery_link_ttl
Seconds a recovery link works.
Default: 3600.
.It Li mail_link_rate_limit , mail_link_rate_window
Messages with a link per address, and the window in seconds.
Over the limit the reply does not change and no message goes out.
Default: 3 per 3600.
.It Li mail_link_source_rate_limit , mail_link_source_rate_window
The same per client address.
Default: 10 per 3600.
.It Li avatars
.Dq enabled
lets each account set a picture on
.Pa /account ,
serves it at
.Pa /avatar/<id>
and names it in
.Pa /session
and
.Pa /api/v1/validate .
A change needs a recent sign-in and mails no notice.
The web server must route
.Pa /avatar/ .
Default:
.Dq disabled .
.It Li avatar_size
The side of a picture in pixels, from 32 to 512.
The account page crops and scales to it, and an upload of any other size is
refused.
Pictures already stored keep their size.
Default: 256.
.It Li avatar_rate_limit , avatar_rate_window
New pictures per account, and the window in seconds.
Default: 10 per 3600.
.El
.Pp
The following fields control rate limiting.
The counts are kept in memory, by each
.Xr signet 1
process: instances that share a
.Va csv_path
and
.Va database_path
each count on their own, so
.Ar n
of them allow
.Ar n
times each limit.
A restart or a
.Dv SIGHUP
reload starts the counts over.
The lockout under
.Va account_lockout_threshold
is kept in the database instead, and shared.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li login_address_limit
Whether failed passwords are counted per address:
.Dq enabled
or
.Dq disabled .
It stops guessing at one account, but anyone who knows an address can block
its password login until the window passes.
Other sign-in methods are not affected.
Default:
.Dq enabled .
.It Li login_rate_limit
Maximum number of failed password attempts per address within the rate window.
An attempt counts from when it starts, so attempts sent together get no more
than this; a success gives its count back and clears the rest.
Must be greater than 0 while the limit is enabled.
Default: 5.
.It Li login_rate_window
Rate window in seconds for login attempts.
Default: 300 (5 minutes).
.It Li login_source_limit
Whether failed passwords are counted per client address:
.Dq enabled
or
.Dq disabled .
It stops one source trying a password across every account.
A client over the limit is refused even with the right password, and signing
in does not clear the count.
Behind a proxy,
.Va trusted_proxies
must list it, or all clients share this budget and one of them can block
everyone.
Default:
.Dq enabled .
With both this and
.Va login_address_limit
disabled,
.Cm serve
warns that nothing limits password guessing.
.It Li login_source_rate_limit
Maximum number of failed password attempts per client address within the
window.
Must be greater than 0 while the limit is enabled.
Default: 20.
.It Li login_source_rate_window
Rate window in seconds for the limit per client address.
Default: 300 (5 minutes).
.It Li account_lockout_threshold
How many failed sign-ins in a row lock an account, so that guessing stops there
.Pq NIST SP 800-63B \(sc5.2.2 .
Wrong passwords, emailed PINs and TOTP or recovery codes count,
for accounts that
exist; the count is in the database, so instances sharing it share it and a
restart keeps it.
Only a finished sign-in clears it: a right password still waiting for its code
does not.
While locked, those methods refuse without checking, with the same answer as a
wrong password, and the owner gets a notice by mail.
A security key still signs in, and password recovery,
.Cm signet user-unlock
or a finished sign-in ends the lock.
When the lock ends, one more failure locks again.
0 switches it off.
Default: 100.
.It Li account_lockout_duration
Minutes a lock by
.Va account_lockout_threshold
lasts.
The email PIN lockout is separate and fixed: three wrong guesses against an
address stop PIN sign-in for it for 30 minutes.
Default: 30.
.It Li email_rate_limit
Maximum number of email PIN requests within the rate window, counted separately
per requester and per target address, and of PIN checks per requester.
Default: 3.
.It Li email_rate_window
Rate window in seconds for email PIN requests.
Default: 60.
.It Li webauthn_rate_limit
Security key ceremony steps allowed per requester in the window.
A sign-in takes two steps.
It is counted per requester and never per address,
so that nobody can lock a user
out of their own key.
Default: 30.
.It Li webauthn_rate_window
Window in seconds for
.Li webauthn_rate_limit .
Default: 60.
.It Li register_rate_limit
Valid self-registration attempts allowed per requester in the window, with
.Va email_verification
disabled.
With it required, the
.Va mail_link
limits apply.
A request refused for a bad address or password does not count.
Default: 5.
.It Li register_rate_window
Window in seconds for
.Li register_rate_limit .
Default: 3600.
.It Li api_rate_limit
Maximum number of API requests within the rate window.
Default: 60.
.It Li api_rate_window
Rate window in seconds for API requests.
Default: 60.
.El
.Pp
The following fields control logging:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li log_level
Not implemented yet: every level is logged.
Log level:
.Dq debug ,
.Dq info ,
.Dq warn ,
.Dq error .
Default:
.Dq info .
.It Li log_syslog
If true, log to syslog instead of standard output.
An error that stops the server goes to standard error and syslog whatever this
says, since the configuration itself may be what failed.
Under
.Xr systemd 1 ,
everything goes to standard output and standard error for the journal to
collect,
and this setting has no effect.
Default: false.
.El
.Pp
The following fields say where WebAuthn applies.
.Va auth_methods.webauthn
switches it on; if these are missing or invalid, it stays off with a warning in
the log.
The older
.Li webauthn_enabled
key is deprecated: false still turns WebAuthn off, and
.Ic serve
warns about it.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li webauthn_rpid
WebAuthn Relying Party ID: a domain name, not an IP address.
Required for WebAuthn; no default.
.It Li webauthn_rp_name
WebAuthn Relying Party display name.
Default:
.Dq Signet .
.It Li webauthn_origins
List of allowed origins for WebAuthn: the exact scheme, host and port of the
page.
Browsers offer security keys only in a secure context, which means HTTPS or
.Li localhost .
The first entry is the default origin for
.Cm signet webauthn-invite
links.
Required for WebAuthn; no default.
.El
.Pp
The following field controls where signet may send the browser after login:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li allowed_redirect_hosts
List of hostnames a cross-host login redirect may target.
A browser on a listed host may also send signet state-changing requests, which
are otherwise refused from any other origin.
Return locations come from the posted
.Va referrer
field, the
.Cm ?referrer=
query parameter or the HTTP
.Ev Referer
header.
Local paths and URLs on the request host are always accepted without being
listed; an absolute URL on a different host is honoured only when its hostname
appears here.
An entry matches the target hostname case-insensitively;
without a port it accepts the scheme's default port only,
with a port it accepts that port only.
IP addresses are valid entries.
IPv6 addresses may be written in full or minified form and match either
spelling in the URL; bracket them
.Pq Li [fd00::5] No / Li [fd00::5]:9443
to carry a port.
Default: empty, so cross-host redirects are refused.
This lets applications protected by signet, e.g. through
.Cm forward_auth ,
send users back after they sign in.
.El
.Pp
The mail configuration.
Signet speaks no mail protocol: like
.Xr mail 1 ,
it runs
.Xr sendmail 8
and leaves delivery, and what may be delivered where, to the system's mail
infrastructure.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li mail.program
The program to run, as
.Li program -i [-f from_address] \-\- recipient
with the message on standard input.
If it exits with a failure, or does not finish within 30 seconds, the failure is
logged.
If empty, messages are written to the process log instead of being sent, PINs
included, and
.Xr signet 1
warns at startup; only use that for development.
Default:
.Pa /usr/sbin/sendmail .
.It Li mail.from_address
The sender's address, used for both the envelope and the
.Dq From
header.
It must be able to receive mail, because bounces go to it: if it cannot,
a message that
fails after being queued is lost, and signet only ever learns that it was
queued.
If empty, the mail system fills it in.
Default: empty.
.It Li mail.from_name
The sender's display name, used only with
.Li mail.from_address .
Default:
.Dq Signet .
.El
.Pp
The external claims hook configuration; the program's protocol is in
.Xr signet-hooks 5 :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li claims_hook.enabled
Whether the external claims hook is enabled.
Default: false.
.It Li claims_hook.program
Path to the claims hook program.
Default: empty.
.It Li claims_hook.timeout_seconds
Timeout in seconds for the claims hook.
Default: 5.
.It Li claims_hook.on_failure
Behavior when the claims hook fails.
Must be
.Dq fail-closed
or
.Dq fail-open .
Default:
.Dq fail-closed .
.It Li claims_hook.refresh_rate_limit
Hook runs that
.Li GET /session?claims=refresh
may start per account within the refresh window.
Past it the stored claims are answered, and the refusal is logged.
Must be greater than 0.
Default: 6.
.It Li claims_hook.refresh_rate_window
Window in seconds for
.Li claims_hook.refresh_rate_limit .
Default: 60.
.El
.Pp
The RADIUS client.
With it on, a password sign-in for a name the CSV file does not have is
checked by RADIUS servers (RFC 2865, over UDP).
A name the file has, in any state, never goes to them, so local accounts work
while the servers are down and their passwords stay on this host.
So a local account shadows a RADIUS one of the same name: when RADIUS names are
mail addresses, an account created in the file for one of them, by
.Cm user-add
or by
.Li self_registration
for whoever can read that mailbox, signs in with its own password and never
reaches the server.
Keep the two sets of names apart, or leave
.Li self_registration
off.
A RADIUS name need not be a mail address; it is sent exactly as typed.
A RADIUS account has no password here, no mailed PIN, recovery or security
key, and gets no account-change notices.
An authenticator app may follow its password as a second factor, as
.Li second_factor
says, and with
.Li second_factor
.Dq required
it adds one at sign-in.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li radius.enabled
Whether RADIUS checks names the CSV file lacks.
It needs
.Li auth_methods.password .
Default: false.
.It Li radius.nas_identifier
The application, sent as
.Li NAS-Identifier
(RFC 2865 section 5.32), so that a server can answer each application
differently.
Required when RADIUS is on.
.It Li radius.servers
The servers, tried in order until one answers.
Each has:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li address
.Ar host
or
.Ar host : Ns Ar port ;
the port defaults to 1812.
.It Li secret , secret_file
The shared secret, or a file holding it; exactly one.
A file is read at start and at each reload, and a final newline is not part of
the secret.
.It Li auth
.Dq pap ,
the default, or
.Dq mschapv2 .
A challenge (below) works with PAP only.
.El
.It Li radius.timeout_seconds
How long each try waits for a reply, 1 to 60.
Default: 3.
.It Li radius.retries
More tries per server after the first, 0 to 10.
A retry keeps the request's identifier and authenticator.
Default: 2.
.It Li radius.group_attributes
The reply attributes whose values are the account's groups:
.Dq Filter-Id ,
.Dq Class ,
an attribute number, or
.Dq vendor: Ns Ar id : Ns Ar type
for a vendor attribute.
Each value is one group; values that are not printable text are left out and
logged.
Default:
.Dq Filter-Id .
.It Li radius.max_session_timeout , radius.max_idle_timeout
The most, in seconds, that an Access-Accept's
.Li Session-Timeout
(RFC 2865 section 5.27) and
.Li Idle-Timeout
(section 5.28) may set a session's lifetime and idle limit to.
They replace
.Li cookie_max_age
and
.Li session_idle_timeout
for that session, longer or shorter, up to these.
0 is Signet's own:
.Li cookie_max_age ,
and
.Li session_idle_timeout
or, when that is 0,
.Li cookie_max_age .
So until these are raised, a server can only shorten a session.
Signet does not send its own limits: RFC 2865 section 5.44 forbids both
attributes in an Access-Request.
Default: 0.
.It Li radius.require_message_authenticator
Drop replies without a
.Li Message-Authenticator
(RFC 3579 section 3.2).
Requests always carry one.
Turning this off exposes sign-ins to forged replies (CVE-2024-3596); do so only
for a server that cannot send it.
Default: true.
.El
.Pp
Each request carries
.Li User-Name ,
the password
.Pq Li User-Password , No or Li MS-CHAP-Challenge No and Li MS-CHAP2-Response ,
.Li NAS-Identifier ,
.Li NAS-Port-Type
Virtual,
.Li Service-Type
Login,
.Li Message-Authenticator ,
and
.Li Calling-Station-Id ,
the client's IP address, when the client has one.
Every reply's Response Authenticator is checked, and an MS-CHAPv2
Access-Accept must carry an
.Li MS-CHAP2-Success
that proves the server knew the password.
.Pp
An Access-Accept signs in, unless a second factor follows.
The session's claims are
.Dq groups ,
or what the claims hook makes of the reply; see
.Xr signet-hooks 5 .
The groups are fixed for the session's life:
a claims refresh gives the hook the reply stored at sign-in.
An Access-Challenge asks for one more answer, with the server's
.Li Reply-Message
as the prompt, up to 3 times in one sign-in.
An Access-Reject counts as a failed sign-in.
When no server gives a valid reply, the sign-in fails with
.Sy 503
and the log names each server's problem.
.Pp
PAP over UDP hides the password with MD5 and the shared secret, and MS-CHAPv2
can be attacked offline from a captured exchange.
Keep RADIUS on a trusted network, or under IPsec.
.Pp
The cookie configuration:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li cookie_name
Name of the authentication cookie.
Each instance on a host needs its own.
Default:
.Dq signetid .
.It Li cookie_domain
Cookie domain.
If empty, the cookie is scoped to the current host.
Default: empty.
.It Li cookie_max_age
Cookie and session lifetime in seconds, from sign-in.
Default: 86400 (24 hours).
.It Li session_idle_timeout
End a session after this many seconds without a request that reads it:
.Cm GET /session ,
.Cm POST /api/v1/validate
or the account page.
A use is written down at most once a minute, or every quarter of the limit when
that is shorter, so a session ends up to that much later than the limit.
0 never ends one for being idle.
Default: 0.
.It Li cookie_secure
Whether the cookie carries the
.Dq Secure
attribute.
Set it to false only for plain-HTTP test deployments; otherwise browsers drop
the cookie.
Default: true.
.It Li cookie_secret
Key that signs session cookies, at least 32 characters.
Required by
.Cm signet serve ,
which refuses to start without it; other commands do not read it.
Generate one with
.Dl openssl rand -hex 32
Instances sharing a database must use the same value, or each rejects the
other's cookies.
Changing it signs everyone out.
.Cm serve
logs a warning when this file is world-readable.
.El
.Pp
The
.Va auth_methods
field controls which authentication methods are enabled:
.Pp
.Dl auth_methods:
.Dl   password: enabled
.Dl   webauthn: disabled
.Dl   email_pin: enabled
.Dl   totp: disabled
.Pp
Each value must be
.Dq enabled
or
.Dq disabled .
Setting
.Li password
to
.Dq disabled
makes
.Cm POST /login
answer 403.
With
.Li totp
enabled, people can add authenticator apps on
.Pa /account ,
and an account with one needs a code after a password or an emailed PIN.
A security key signs in alone.
Disabling it stops both, so such an account then signs in with no code, and
.Cm serve
warns if keys exist.
The keys are encrypted with a key derived from
.Va cookie_secret ,
so changing that makes them all unreadable, and people must add them again.
The enabled methods are exposed to the login template as
.Va .PasswordEnabled ,
.Va .WebAuthnEnabled ,
.Va .EmailPINEnabled
and
.Va .TOTPEnabled ;
see
.Xr signet-template 5 .
.Sh CSV FILE FORMAT
The CSV file at
.Va csv_path
contains user accounts in the following format:
.Pp
.Dl email,password_hash,hash_algorithm,flags,otp_secret
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
Each line represents one user.
.It
Lines starting with
.Dq #
are comments and are ignored.
Comments, blank lines and rows with too few fields are kept when
.Xr signet 1
rewrites the file.
.It
The
.Va password_hash
column holds the password hash, or
.Dq !
for an account without a password.
.It
The
.Va hash_algorithm
column must be
.Dq bcrypt
or
.Dq argon2id .
.It
The
.Va flags
column is a pipe-delimited list of flags:
.Bl -dash -offset indent
.It
.Dq self_registered
\- user self-registered
.It
.Dq approved
\- user has been approved; an account with
.Dq self_registered
and without this flag cannot sign in while
.Va self_registration_approval
is
.Dq required
.It
.Dq mfa_enabled
\- multi-factor authentication is enabled
.It
.Dq locked
\- account cannot sign in by any method; existing sessions stay valid until
they expire
.El
.It
The
.Va otp_secret
column is unused: TOTP keys are in the database.
It may be omitted.
.It
A new file is created readable by its owner only.
Addresses cannot contain whitespace or control characters, or start with
.Dq # .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A minimal configuration:
.Pp
.Dl listen: :9090
.Dl cookie_secret: <output of openssl rand -hex 32>
.Pp
A full configuration:
.Pp
.D1 in
.Bd -literal -offset indent
listen: ":9090"
socket_mode: "0660"
cookie_secret: "<output of openssl rand -hex 32>"
templates_dir: "/etc/signet/templates"
static_dir: "/etc/signet/static"
database_path: "/var/lib/signet/sessions.db"
csv_path: "/var/lib/signet/users.csv"

password_hash_algorithm: "argon2id"
bcrypt_cost: 12
argon2id_time: 1
argon2id_memory: 65536
argon2id_parallelism: 4
argon2id_salt_length: 16
argon2id_key_length: 32

self_registration: "disabled"
self_registration_approval: "required"
email_verification: "required"
multi_device: "enabled"
password_recovery: "disabled"
# public_url: "https://signet.example.com"
# base_path: "/comments-auth"

login_address_limit: "enabled"
login_rate_limit: 5
login_rate_window: 300
login_source_limit: "enabled"
login_source_rate_limit: 20
login_source_rate_window: 300
account_lockout_threshold: 100
account_lockout_duration: 30


webauthn_rpid: "signet.example.com"
webauthn_rp_name: "Signet"
webauthn_origins:
  - "https://signet.example.com"

allowed_redirect_hosts:
  - "app.example.com"

mail:
  from_address: "noreply@signet.example.com"

claims_hook:
  enabled: false

log_level: "info"
log_syslog: false
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr signet 1 ,
.Xr signet-hooks 5 ,
.Xr signet-template 5 ,
.Xr sendmail 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
configuration format was introduced in 2026.
